    "content": "\"Epitaph\" launched Hannes Grossmann's career as one of modern extreme metal's defining drummers, and the trajectory from Epitaph (2004) to Obscura's Cosmogenesis (2009) and beyond is one of the most instructive evolutions in tech death.\n\n**From Necrophagist to Obscura:**\nOn Epitaph (2004), Grossmann played within Necrophagist's pure technical death metal framework — fast, precise, vocabulary-driven drumming that prioritized technical execution within Muhammed Suiçmez's neoclassical compositions. On Cosmogenesis (2009), the same technical capability was redeployed in a progressive context where compositional sophistication and atmospheric depth mattered as much as raw velocity. The arc from Epitaph to Cosmogenesis traces Grossmann's maturation from genre executor to genre architect.\n\n**The Gear Evolution:**\nGrossmann's relationship with Meinl deepened across this period. By Cosmogenesis, the Byzance cymbal setup had become his signature voice — one of the most recognizable cymbal tonal palettes in modern tech death. On Epitaph, that voice was in its developing stage, transitioning from Pure Alloy elements toward the mature Byzance configuration.\n\n**Beyond:**\nThe albums that followed in Obscura's catalog — Omnivium (2011), Akroasis (2016), Diluvium (2018) — and Grossmann's parallel work with Alkaloid (founded 2015) all build on the technical foundation established on Epitaph and refined across Cosmogenesis. Epitaph remains the foundational document of that career.",
